{"step_index":7678,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T18:01:27Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-25T18:01:27Z\nCompleted At: 2026-09-25T18:01:32Z\nThe search for \"how to upload to google one drive from linux server headless\" returned the following summary:\nTo upload files to Google Drive from a headless Linux server, the most reliable and widely recommended tool is **rclone**[1][2]. Because rclone requires an OAuth authorization flow (which involves a web browser), you typically perform the initial setup on your local computer and then transfer the configuration to your server[3][4].\n\n### Recommended Approach: Using Rclone\n\n#### 1. Install Rclone\nOn your Linux server, install rclone using your package manager or the official script[1][3]:\n```bash\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install rclone\n```\n\n#### 2. Perform Authorization (The \"Headless\" Trick)\nSince your server has no browser, the easiest way to authorize rclone is to do it on your local machine (where you have a browser) and move the configuration file[3][4].\n\n1.  **On your local machine:** Install rclone and run `rclone authorize \"drive\"`.\n2.  This will open a browser window. Log in to your Google account and grant the necessary permissions[2][5].\n3.  Once authorized, rclone will output a JSON configuration string (your \"token\"). Copy this string[2].\n4.  **On your server:** Run `rclone config` to create a new remote[1][6].\n5.  When prompted for \"Use web browser to automatically authenticate,\" select **No**[7][8].\n6.  Paste the token you copied from your local machine when asked[2].\n\n*Alternatively, you can use an **SSH Tunnel** to perform the browser-based authorization directly through your server[9][8]:*\n*   SSH into your server with port forwarding: `ssh -L 53682:127.0.0.1:53682 user@your-server-ip`[8].\n*   Run `rclone config` on the server and follow the prompts. When it gives you a URL, open it on your local browser; the tunnel will redirect the authentication back to your server[8].\n\n#### 3. Uploading Files\nOnce configured (e.g., your remote is named `gdrive`), you can upload files easily[6]:\n\n*   **Copy a file:**\n    ```bash\n    rcl\n<truncated 2275 bytes>\ncom/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFuXW-u78gL-8qf5CGoLGY8QewFHOr31uvyjYsurFaNrBPCg3nxMJEqgt3J3Qzhlke2jsgMoynclusKmSecbz25KrM3fxWo2mqnFgVtqi6PoiXyV29_KKgWKhQ46yg2ieGzjUjhqKuh)\n[6] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHfZLEEBWUmgTsdxlNQWnRrNYCNjEutUMW5FtgAWFx_gbppMeO5Z7nBYUVmsOpd1m5aZRB2MCaRaxuhKwS-znpZ0rqM5ey0rPGd86r7BF2vkBVaZ7LNjP9OgnDIvZf0Q-FVGEPy0_4Ju9OKwueLauKeSzkHpXOEsvyHwsYKu74nxsoBjujIcCkqM315Exi46A==)\n[7] [rclone.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH87IyJufFw6ue6eyGp7i3QWdPT-SOEkq9GsazqY117K9KrCGMrRwS2JGDC1KHohlvY0npqQjS_lB42bHIBoN0mg028AnpSPL3nz11kEnH06dGLEP7T1A==)\n[8] [surajmishra.co.in](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEAt_Wflid1ccKllH2jInZ8_9ATQZ2RVCBipPa_tMoXEf81Lze1BFokFXaBnXhoMulFNe3pxbpg8-B0kYUB_ZP46rmgEhOycy-HHuC_zGWdDt6EUmgeeOlGqQwJwoxmvmCmTOOl5wISW6MpSkxho9p3HRRZKp3p2LHb1zfGWY9yBtjtq0wZvqQDxjo7uFo=)\n[9] [medium.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEJNPz9ONtB6AqKTaAVgJU5W1iGwq_IwwMDU-f_LQz3j5TbTxwdu-znspZAYgR7lezbBmcVe_IU2_pCkX_agmwP2aAsqxcZWBe4n0uGZhKsJCMyAyjb8oTORyKd9_ctBC4mBYyrGNnu77CQsWrdmd4TRiuGN9xjJvFy1vH0Isi4dUJcoshB1QnOeT-pfz4-sf0gwPzok1THvwPCUv-YX8KQOLUuTAJw0n-P5U89vN7G5CZqjf25Dzf7uK6HWQ91)\n[10] [askubuntu.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGLBGBmtWp4xcwXy5OmcNL0SXtNO_Uuza__4wNuJCUMiC985_LAt7yxabBP-HIvxeZbzvyMcmd3AyiVm5JSEX__9sHHM_fYU2W8muNhWJ7ZOyM_0a0eUParF96ChI82vwm41x1PRrEyY9OL2qzCCLJPIm6uSS81XtmG_CxjRidLQ_4=)\n[11] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEsxkQXQQm0xS-AzwtWcqJZMwHBdbxoibb6f9XJszPJay7afNuCD2bwC-1_wpN60P0ASeS7vuvDf4jA56Re3HjJmYk8xi5JPwlyB7FQ782JiJGtC045Vyx