{"step_index":7680,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T18:01:32Z","thinking":"Remote server access without a local browser necessitates either manual token transfer after authorization on another device or tunneling the authorization port. The initial tunneling approach faces a constraint: Google OAuth restricts redirects for desktop clients to a specific local address. An alternative method depends on whether the user has a terminal app available on their Android device.\n\n\nIraq Bhai's access to a terminal app suggests Google Cloud Shell might be open and pre-authenticated. If so, command-line tools for cloud storage could be utilized directly, potentially bypassing the need for a web browser-based authorization flow. Another approach could involve a Python script with OAuth, but this requires web redirects.\n\nA service account faces storage quota limitations, but these could be circumvented by using a shared drive, if one exists.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"python3 -c \\\"\\nimport subprocess\\n\\np = subprocess.Popen(['rclone', 'config', 'reconnect', 'gdrive:'], stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True)\\nout, _ = p.communicate(input='n\\\\n', timeout=5)\\nprint(out)\\n\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"/home/azureuser\"","RequestedTerminalID":"\"\"","RunPersistent":"false","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Testing rclone reconnect prompt\"","toolSummary":"\"Test rclone reconnect prompts\""}}]}
